{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>אֶת הַגָּמָל כִּי מַעֲלֵה גֵרָה הוּא.</b> It should have said, 'for he does not split the hoof' — for this is the principal reason for its impurity. And the same difficulty applies in the case of the שָׁפָן and the אַרְנֶבֶת. And in the case of the חֲזִיר it says: ״כִּי מַפְרִיס פַּרְסָה הוּא״ — it should have said, 'for he does not bring up the cud.' Why, then, does it begin in all of them with their sign of purity? And the explanation of this matter is that the sign of purity that is in each of them adds impurity upon their impurity — in the manner that they said concerning Esau (Bereshit Rabbah 65:1) that he was likened to the חֲזִיר, which stretches out its hoofs to show as though it is kosher while its innards are full of violence and deceit. And this indicates concerning everyone whose inside is not like his outside — in the manner of the hypocrites who show themselves to be kosher, and they are without doubt worse than the outright wicked man, whose inside and outside are equally evil, as Rashi explained on the verse ״וְלֹא יָכְלוּ דַּבְּרוֹ לְשָׁלוֹם״ (Genesis 37:4). And therefore the split hoof in the חֲזִיר is a sign of impurity, because with this hoof it can mislead people and show as though it is kosher. And the same applies in the reverse manner to the גָּמָל, שָׁפָן, and אַרְנֶבֶת. And even though all these traits do not apply to animals, nevertheless they produce this bad disposition in the body of one who eats them, for כָּל אוֹכְלֵיהֶם יֶאְשָׁמוּ (after Jeremiah 2:3) — to become of the classes of the hypocrites who show themselves to be kosher like Esau and his companions." "And with this, what they expounded (Vayikra Rabbah 13:5) is settled: ״אֶת הַגָּמָל״ — this is Babylon, as it says (Psalms 137:8): ״בַּת בָּבֶל הַשְּׁדוּדָה אַשְׁרֵי שֶׁיְּשַׁלֶּם לָךְ גְּמוּלֵךְ שֶׁגָּמַלְתְּ לָנוּ״. ״וְאֶת הַשָּׁפָן״ — this is Media, for it brings up the cud, for it raises up righteous men, as it says (Esther 2:21): ״וּמָרְדֳּכַי יוֹשֵׁב בְּשַׁעַר הַמֶּלֶךְ״. ״אַרְנֶבֶת״ — this is Greece, for it brings up the cud: Alexander, when he saw Simeon the Righteous, stood on his feet and blessed him, etc. (see Yoma 69a). ״הַחֲזִיר״ — this is Edom, which splits the hoof, showing itself to be kosher, but does not chew the cud, for it does not raise righteous men but rather kills them, etc. And one may wonder about this midrash: what did it see to take all these verses out of their plain sense? But surely its intent was to resolve the four instances of ״כִּי״ that it mentioned, which made the sign of purity into the reason for impurity. And without doubt the intent of the verse is to allude to the trait of the hypocrites — that their sign of purity adds impurity to them, and this does not apply to animals at all but only to man. Therefore it means to say that everyone who eats of them receives their nature, for the eaten thing turns into the nature of the eater. And it brought proof from what is well-known, that all these four kingdoms that consume from these four species all received their nature — that they show themselves as though they are kosher, as if their intent were to do good for Israel, while their innards are evil and bitter, for all their thoughts are only evil all the day. And Israel did not guard themselves from them when they saw that they raise up some righteous men — and in truth they did so for their own benefit, while their innards were haters of Israel." "And what they said, ״אֶת הַגָּמָל״ — this is Babylon: its intent is not to interpret the verse and take it out of its plain sense, but to tell you that the form of the camel is found in the kingdom of Babylon. For just as the camel brings up the cud, and from this side it appears kosher, so too the kingdom of Babylon showed themselves as bestowers of kindnesses upon Israel. And Scripture says (Psalms 137:8): ״אַשְׁרֵי שֶׁיְּשַׁלֶּם לָךְ גְּמוּלֵךְ שֶׁגָּמַלְתְּ לָנוּ״, etc. — to tell you that that recompense was on the side of deception. And likewise Media and Greece, who would draw the righteous men close — like Mordecai and Simeon the Righteous — and all of it was on the side of deception. And likewise Edom, who act like the deed of the חֲזִיר, which shows its hoofs as though kosher — to say that they do not stretch out their hands to robbery to take the money of Israel by force, yet they kill them. And there is no need to elaborate further on this. And on the side of allegory it made a link from גָּמָל to Babylon, and שָׁפָן to Media, etc. Nevertheless, its intent is never to interpret the verses outside their plain sense."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:35:51.223366478Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}